{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And YHWH your Elohim will circumcise your heart</b>. This is what the Sages said (Shabbat 104a): one who comes to purify himself is assisted. He promises you that you will return to Him with all your heart, and He will help you.\n\nAnd this matter — which I shall state — appears from Scripture: for from the time of Creation, free choice was in the hand of man to do as he wishes, righteous or wicked, and throughout the entire era of the Torah it remains so, in order that they have merit through their choice of good and punishment through their desire for evil. But in the days of the Messiah, the choice of good will become their nature; the heart will not crave that which is unfit, nor will it desire it at all. And this is the circumcision mentioned here, for desire and craving are the foreskin of the heart, and circumcision of the heart is that one will not crave and not desire. And man in that time will return to what he was before the sin of the first man, when he did by his nature what was proper to do, and he had no desire for a thing and its opposite, as I explained in the portion of Genesis (Genesis 2:9).\n\nAnd this is what Scripture says in Jeremiah (Jeremiah 31:30–31): \"Behold, days are coming, says YHWH, and I will cut with the house of Israel and the house of Judah a new covenant, not like the covenant that I cut with their fathers,\" etc. \"For this is the covenant that I will cut with the house of Israel after those days: I will put My Torah within them and on their heart I will write it\" — and this is the nullification of the evil inclination and the heart's doing its proper deed by nature. Therefore He further said (ibid. vv. 32–33): \"And I will be for them for an Elohim and they will be for Me for a people, and they shall no longer teach each man his fellow and each man his brother, saying: Know YHWH, for all of them shall know Me, from the smallest of them to the greatest of them.\" And it is known that the inclination of man's heart is evil from his youth, and they need to be taught — rather, their inclination will be nullified entirely in that time.\n\nSo too it is stated in Ezekiel (Ezekiel 36:26–27): \"And I will give you a new heart and a new spirit I will put within you,\" etc., \"and I will do that which in My statutes you shall walk.\" And the new heart hints to his nature, and the spirit to desire and will.\n\nAnd this is what our Sages said (Shabbat 151b): \"And the years arrive of which you will say: I have no desire in them\" (Ecclesiastes 12:1) — these are the days of the Messiah, in which there is neither merit nor liability. For in the days of the Messiah man will have no desire, but rather he will do by his nature the proper deed; therefore there is in them neither merit nor liability, for merit and liability depend on desire."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:57:04.712438743Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}